              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "If you just finished *The Expanse*, you\u2019ve completed one of the modern masterpieces of space opera! Saying goodbye to the crew of the *Rocinante* can leave a massive void, but there are several incredible books and series that can scratch that exact itch. \n\nDepending on what you loved most about *The Expanse*, here are the best directions to go next:\n\n### 1. If you want the exact same storytelling magic:\n**_The Mercy of Gods_ by James S.A. Corey** (The Captive's War Series)\n* **The Vibe:** Humanity caught in the gears of a massive, ancient alien empire.\n* **Why you\u2019ll love it:** This is the brand-new sci-fi series by the exact same writing duo behind *The Expanse*. Instead of gritty, near-future Solar System politics, they take a leap into deep space. The story begins on a human colony planet that is suddenly conquered by an ancient alien empire called the Carryx. Humans are taken captive, and a group of scientists must figure out how to survive and remain useful to their captors. It features the same brilliant, character-driven multi-POV writing and masterful world-building. Best of all, the sequel, ***The Faith of Beasts***, was just released, so you can jump right into the next chapter of the saga.\n\n### 2. If you loved the \"misfit crew on a spaceship\" vibe:\n**_Shards of Earth_ by Adrian Tchaikovsky** (The Final Architecture Trilogy)\n* **The Vibe:** *The Expanse* meets *Mass Effect*, with incredible alien species and cosmic horror.\n* **Why you\u2019ll love it:** If your favorite part of *The Expanse* was the tight-knit found family of the *Roci* getting caught in conflicts way over their heads, this is the perfect next step. The story follows the crew of a salvage ship who discover the terrifying return of the \"Architects\"\u2014moon-sized crystalline entities that warp and destroy inhabited planets. Tchaikovsky is a master of writing deeply distinct, fascinating alien cultures, and the crew chemistry is phenomenal.\n\n### 3. If you loved the gritty Solar System politics and action:\n**_Red Rising_ by Pierce Brown**\n* **The Vibe:** High-octane space opera, Roman-inspired empire, and brutal faction warfare.\n* **Why you\u2019ll love it:** *Red Rising* starts as a dystopian rebellion on Mars and rapidly evolves into a massive, solar-system-spanning war. Society is split into a strict, color-coded caste system (the low-class \"Reds\" mining Mars' interior have a lot of thematic crossover with the Belters). It is incredibly fast-paced, filled with political betrayal, massive space battles, and some of the most intense, emotional character arcs in modern sci-fi.\n\n### 4. If you loved the realistic physics and cosmic horror:\n**_Revelation Space_ by Alastair Reynolds**\n* **The Vibe:** Ultra-hard sci-fi, ancient mysteries, and cold, realistic space travel.\n* **Why you\u2019ll love it:** If you appreciated how *The Expanse* respected orbital mechanics, G-forces, and the vacuum of space, Reynolds (a former ESA scientist) is the gold standard. In this universe, there is no faster-than-light travel; ships must travel at relativistic speeds, meaning voyages take decades. It blends hard physics with a terrifying, gothic mystery surrounding the extinction of an ancient alien race, striking a very similar chord to the \"Ring Builders\" storyline. \n\n### 5. If you loved the political intrigue and diplomacy:\n**_A Memory Called Empire_ by Arkady Martine**\n* **The Vibe:** High-stakes political thriller, cultural assimilation, and murder mystery.\n* **Why you\u2019ll love it:** If you lived for the scenes of Chrisjen Avasarala maneuvering through the halls of power, you will adore this book. It follows Mahit Dzmare, a young ambassador from a small independent mining station who is sent to the capital of a massive, planet-conquering empire. When she arrives, she discovers her predecessor was murdered, and she must navigate a deadly web of imperial court politics, espionage, and impending civil war to keep her home station from being swallowed whole.",
